ROOT=Path.cwd();ART=ROOT/"articles";BACK=ROOT/"backup-bloco-07-artigos";BACK.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
DATA=[{'filename': 'como-elegir-alfombras-para-casa.html', 'title': 'How to Choose Rugs for Every Room in Your Home', 'category': 'HOME DECOR', 'meta': 'Learn how to choose rugs for each room by considering size, furniture placement, materials, pile height, cleaning, safety, color, pattern, and daily use.', 'intro': 'Choosing a rug involves more than selecting a pattern. Size, material, cleaning, foot traffic, furniture placement, texture, and safety determine whether it truly improves comfort and decor.', 'body': "\n<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<ul><li>Introduction</li><li>Measure Before You Buy</li><li>Choose the Right Rug Size for a Living Room</li><li>Plan a Dining Room Rug</li><li>Choose Rugs for Bedrooms and Hallways</li><li>Match the Rug to Daily Use</li><li>Understand Materials and Construction</li><li>Think About Texture and Pile Height</li><li>Plan Cleaning and Maintenance</li><li>Use Rug Pads and Improve Safety</li><li>Coordinate Color and Pattern</li><li>Choose Shape With the Furniture</li><li>Shop More Carefully</li><li>Frequently Asked Questions</li><li>Conclusion</li></ul>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>Choosing a rug involves much more than finding an attractive pattern. Size, material, construction, pile height, cleaning requirements, foot traffic, furniture placement, and the floor underneath all influence whether a rug improves a room in everyday use.</p>\n<p>A well-chosen rug can make a seating area feel connected, add warmth underfoot, reduce the visual hardness of large floor surfaces, and introduce color or texture. A poorly sized or impractical rug, however, can create awkward proportions, interfere with doors, or become difficult to maintain.</p>\n<h2>Measure Before You Buy</h2>\n<p>Measure the room and the furniture arrangement rather than relying only on the room's total dimensions. Note doors, floor vents, fireplaces, built-ins, and any narrow circulation routes that should remain clear.</p>\n<p>Use painter's tape to mark the proposed rug outline on the floor. Walk around it, open doors, move chairs, and view the outline from different parts of the room. This simple test reveals scale problems that are difficult to judge from a product photograph.</p>\n<p>Record the maximum and minimum dimensions that can work before shopping. Rug sizes are often standardized, so knowing your acceptable range makes comparison easier and reduces impulse purchases.</p>\n<h2>Choose the Right Rug Size for a Living Room</h2>\n<p>In a living room, the rug should visually connect the main seating pieces. Depending on the layout, all furniture legs may sit on the rug, or only the front legs of sofas and chairs may rest on it. Consistency is more important than following one universal rule.</p>\n<p>A rug that floats far inside the seating arrangement can make the furniture appear disconnected. Before choosing a smaller rug to save money, test its actual footprint with tape and decide whether the proportions still support the group.</p>\n<p>Leave enough exposed flooring around the rug for circulation and to keep edges away from doors or other obstacles. The ideal border depends on the room rather than a fixed measurement.</p>\n<h2>Plan a Dining Room Rug</h2>\n<p>A dining rug must remain large enough when chairs are pulled away from the table. If rear chair legs repeatedly drop off the edge, the rug can become annoying and unstable during meals.</p>\n<p>Measure the table, then test the chair movement around every side. Include the positions used when someone is sitting down, not only the neat arrangement when all chairs are pushed in.</p>\n<p>Consider spills, crumbs, and cleaning frequency. Very deep pile can trap debris, while delicate materials may be unsuitable for a dining area that is used every day.</p>\n<h2>Choose Rugs for Bedrooms and Hallways</h2>\n<p>In a bedroom, a large rug can extend beyond the bed to provide a soft landing area, or smaller runners can be placed where people actually step. The arrangement should feel proportional to the bed and leave doors operating freely.</p>\n<p>Hallway runners need particular attention to width, edge stability, and pile height. A thick rug can interfere with a door or become a trip hazard in a narrow passage.</p>\n<p>Measure the clear floor area rather than assuming a standard runner will fit. Keep transitions to stairs and adjoining rooms safe and visually tidy.</p>\n<h2>Match the Rug to Daily Use</h2>\n<p>Foot traffic should influence the choice. An entryway or family room generally needs a rug that tolerates frequent use, while a low-traffic bedroom may accommodate a softer or more delicate option.</p>\n<p>Homes with children or pets often benefit from materials and constructions that are easier to vacuum and clean. Think about shedding, claws, tracked dirt, and the kinds of spills that realistically occur in the room.</p>\n<p>For any area exposed to moisture, confirm that the product is intended for those conditions. A rug that remains damp can deteriorate and may affect the surface underneath.</p>\n<h2>Understand Materials and Construction</h2>\n<p>Wool, cotton, plant fibers, and synthetic fibers behave differently. Wool is often valued for resilience and warmth but requires appropriate care. Cotton rugs may be lighter, and some products are washable when their care label permits it.</p>\n<p>Jute and sisal provide natural texture, but their response to stains and moisture should be considered before placing them in demanding areas. Synthetic fibers vary widely and can offer useful stain resistance or easier maintenance depending on the product.</p>\n<p>Do not choose by fiber name alone. Construction, density, backing, dye, and manufacturing quality can make two rugs made from the same general material perform very differently.</p>\n<h2>Think About Texture and Pile Height</h2>\n<p>Texture changes both appearance and maintenance. A plush rug can feel comfortable in a bedroom, while a flatter surface may be easier to manage under dining chairs or in busy circulation areas.</p>\n<p>Check clearance under doors and furniture. Even a small increase in thickness can prevent a door from opening smoothly or cause lightweight furniture to sit unevenly.</p>\n<p>If anyone in the household uses mobility equipment or needs particularly clear walking surfaces, prioritize stable, low-profile solutions and secure edges.</p>\n<h2>Plan Cleaning and Maintenance</h2>\n<p>Read the care instructions before purchasing. Some rugs can be spot cleaned, some require professional cleaning, and some smaller products may be washable. The correct method depends on the specific construction.</p>\n<p>Consider whether you can realistically move the rug for cleaning. A very large rug under heavy furniture may require more effort than a smaller piece, even if its surface is easy to vacuum.</p>\n<p>Regular vacuuming and prompt attention to spills can help preserve many rugs, but always follow manufacturer guidance for products and techniques.</p>\n<h2>Use Rug Pads and Improve Safety</h2>\n<p>A compatible rug pad can reduce movement, add cushioning, and help keep edges stable. Choose a pad designed for both the rug and the type of flooring underneath.</p>\n<p>Do not assume every pad is safe for every floor finish. Certain materials can interact with coatings, so check flooring and rug-pad recommendations.</p>\n<p>Inspect corners and edges periodically. Curling, movement, or damage should be addressed rather than ignored, especially in busy routes.</p>\n<h2>Coordinate Color and Pattern</h2>\n<p>A rug can repeat colors already present in the room or introduce contrast. If upholstery, curtains, and walls already contain strong patterns, a quieter rug may provide balance. In a neutral room, a more expressive design can become a focal point.</p>\n<p>Look at samples in the actual light whenever possible. Natural and artificial lighting can change color significantly, and screens do not reproduce every shade or texture accurately.</p>\n<p>You do not need to match every color exactly. Repeating one or two tones from existing furniture or textiles is often enough to create a visual relationship.</p>\n<h2>Choose Shape With the Furniture</h2>\n<p>Rectangular rugs are versatile, round rugs can reinforce a circular table or define a compact conversation area, and runners suit many narrow spaces. The shape should respond to architecture and furniture rather than trend alone.</p>\n<p>A round rug can soften a room dominated by straight lines, but it still needs enough scale to relate clearly to the furniture placed around it.</p>\n<p>Unusual shapes can work as accents, although they may be harder to position or replace later. Test the outline before committing.</p>\n<h2>Shop More Carefully</h2>\n<p>Set a budget before comparing designs and include any rug pad, delivery, or professional cleaning requirements in the real cost. A durable rug suited to daily use can offer better value than repeated inexpensive replacements.</p>\n<p>When buying online, study exact dimensions, material composition, pile height, backing, care instructions, and return policies. Product photographs may make a rug look larger or show colors differently from your room.</p>\n<p>If samples are available, compare them with the floor, sofa, curtains, and other materials that will remain. Keep the sample in the room through different times of day before making the final decision.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>How do I know whether a rug is too small?</h3><p>Mark its dimensions on the floor with tape. If it fails to connect the main furniture group, leaves dining chairs falling off the edge, or looks isolated from the arrangement, a larger size may work better.</p>\n<h3>What type of rug is easiest to maintain?</h3><p>There is no single answer for every home. Look for a construction and material compatible with your traffic, pets, children, cleaning routine, and the manufacturer's care instructions.</p>\n<h3>Should a rug match the sofa exactly?</h3><p>No. The rug can repeat one or two colors, provide contrast, or introduce texture. Exact matching is not required for a coordinated room.</p>\n<h3>Do I always need a rug pad?</h3><p>It depends on the rug, floor, and manufacturer guidance, but a compatible pad can improve stability and cushioning. Make sure the pad is appropriate for the flooring finish.</p>\n<h2>Review the Rug After Placement</h2><p>Once the rug is in place, use the room normally for several days. Check whether chairs move comfortably, doors clear the pile, edges stay flat, and the rug still feels proportional from the main viewpoints.</p><p>If the arrangement feels crowded or disconnected, adjust furniture placement before assuming that more decoration is needed. Small positioning changes can improve the relationship between the rug and the rest of the room.</p><h2>Review the Rug After Placement</h2><p>Once the rug is in place, use the room normally for several days. Check whether chairs move comfortably, doors clear the pile, edges stay flat, and the rug still feels proportional from the main viewpoints.</p><p>If the arrangement feels crowded or disconnected, adjust furniture placement before assuming that more decoration is needed. Small positioning changes can improve the relationship between the rug and the rest of the room.</p><h2>Review the Rug After Placement</h2><p>Once the rug is in place, use the room normally for several days. Check whether chairs move comfortably, doors clear the pile, edges stay flat, and the rug still feels proportional from the main viewpoints.</p><p>If the arrangement feels crowded or disconnected, adjust furniture placement before assuming that more decoration is needed. Small positioning changes can improve the relationship between the rug and the rest of the room.</p><h2>Review the Rug After Placement</h2><p>Once the rug is in place, use the room normally for several days. Check whether chairs move comfortably, doors clear the pile, edges stay flat, and the rug still feels proportional from the main viewpoints.</p><p>If the arrangement feels crowded or disconnected, adjust furniture placement before assuming that more decoration is needed. Small positioning changes can improve the relationship between the rug and the rest of the room.</p><h2>Review the Rug After Placement</h2><p>Once the rug is in place, use the room normally for several days. Check whether chairs move comfortably, doors clear the pile, edges stay flat, and the rug still feels proportional from the main viewpoints.</p><p>If the arrangement feels crowded or disconnected, adjust furniture placement before assuming that more decoration is needed. Small positioning changes can improve the relationship between the rug and the rest of the room.</p><h2>Review the Rug After Placement</h2><p>Once the rug is in place, use the room normally for several days. Check whether chairs move comfortably, doors clear the pile, edges stay flat, and the rug still feels proportional from the main viewpoints.</p><p>If the arrangement feels crowded or disconnected, adjust furniture placement before assuming that more decoration is needed. Small positioning changes can improve the relationship between the rug and the rest of the room.</p><h2>Review the Rug After Placement</h2><p>Once the rug is in place, use the room normally for several days. Check whether chairs move comfortably, doors clear the pile, edges stay flat, and the rug still feels proportional from the main viewpoints.</p><p>If the arrangement feels crowded or disconnected, adjust furniture placement before assuming that more decoration is needed. Small positioning changes can improve the relationship between the rug and the rest of the room.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>The best rug for a home is one that fits both the physical space and the way the room is used. Measure carefully, test the outline, consider furniture movement, and choose materials that match realistic cleaning and traffic conditions.</p>\n<p>Color and pattern matter, but function should come first. When size, safety, maintenance, texture, and visual balance are considered together, a rug can improve comfort and help the entire room feel more coherent.</p>\n"}, {'filename': 'como-elegir-colores-para-casa.html', 'title': 'How to Choose Colors for Your Home and Create a Balanced Palette', 'category': 'HOME DECOR', 'meta': 'Learn how to choose a balanced home color palette by evaluating natural and artificial light, undertones, fixed materials, room function, paint finish, and full-size samples.', 'intro': 'Choosing colors for a home works best when light, fixed materials, undertones, room function, maintenance, and the relationship between connected spaces are considered together.', 'body': "\n<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<ul><li>Introduction</li><li>Observe Natural and Artificial Light</li><li>Identify Fixed Materials</li><li>Understand Undertones</li><li>Build a Coherent Palette</li><li>Use Proportion Instead of Rigid Rules</li><li>Adapt Color to Each Room</li><li>Test Paint Before Committing</li><li>Coordinate Open and Connected Spaces</li><li>Consider Finish and Maintenance</li><li>Use Dark and Strong Colors Carefully</li><li>Plan a Practical Selection Process</li><li>Frequently Asked Questions</li><li>Conclusion</li></ul>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>Choosing colors for a home is easier when the decision begins with the real space rather than a trend chart. Light, flooring, wood, stone, furniture, room size, and daily use all influence how a color appears and whether the palette feels balanced.</p>\n<p>A useful palette does not require every room to look identical. The objective is continuity: repeated tones, related undertones, and intentional transitions that allow each room to have its own character while still belonging to the same home.</p>\n<h2>Observe Natural and Artificial Light</h2>\n<p>Study each room in the morning, afternoon, and evening. A wall that appears warm in direct sunlight may look cooler or duller when the sun moves away.</p>\n<p>Orientation, window size, exterior reflections, curtains, and nearby buildings can all affect natural light. Do not assume that a paint color seen in another house will behave the same way in yours.</p>\n<p>Artificial lighting also changes perception. Compare samples under the lamps you normally use at night, not only under store lighting or daylight.</p>\n<h2>Identify Fixed Materials</h2>\n<p>Before choosing wall colors, list the surfaces that are likely to remain: flooring, countertops, tiles, stone, large furniture, built-ins, and significant wood finishes. These elements already form part of the palette.</p>\n<p>Place paint samples next to those materials rather than evaluating them against an empty white wall. A beige that looks neutral alone may reveal a pink, yellow, or gray tendency beside the floor.</p>\n<p>If several fixed materials compete with one another, a quieter wall color can help connect them instead of adding another strong element.</p>\n<h2>Understand Undertones</h2>\n<p>Two colors that look almost identical on a small card can behave very differently in a room because of undertones. Whites may lean yellow, gray, blue, green, or pink, and similar variations occur in beige, gray, and other neutrals.</p>\n<p>Compare several samples side by side and against the materials that will remain. The differences often become clearer through comparison.</p>\n<p>Undertones do not need to match perfectly, but obvious conflicts can make a palette feel accidental. Repeating related warm or cool tendencies can create continuity.</p>\n<h2>Build a Coherent Palette</h2>\n<p>Start with a dominant neutral or foundational color, then choose a small number of supporting tones and accents. This creates enough structure to make later decisions easier.</p>\n<p>Repeat selected colors in different rooms through walls, textiles, furniture, art, or smaller details. Repetition does not mean using the same amount everywhere.</p>\n<p>Keep a record of paint names, codes, finishes, and samples. This is useful when touching up a wall or extending the palette to another room later.</p>\n<h2>Use Proportion Instead of Rigid Rules</h2>\n<p>Popular decorating formulas can be useful as a starting point, but they are not laws. The amount of each color should respond to the room, architecture, furniture, and desired emphasis.</p>\n<p>Large surfaces naturally have more visual weight. A wall color may dominate even when it is quiet, while a vivid accent can be effective in a much smaller quantity.</p>\n<p>Choose one or two main focal points rather than making every surface compete for attention. Visual hierarchy usually creates a calmer result.</p>\n<h2>Adapt Color to Each Room</h2>\n<p>Bedrooms, living rooms, kitchens, work areas, and hallways have different functions and light conditions. A color should support the room's use rather than follow a universal recommendation.</p>\n<p>In a small room, light colors can reflect more light, but dark colors are not automatically forbidden. A deeper tone can create intimacy when the lighting and furnishings support it.</p>\n<p>Busy family spaces may benefit from finishes and colors that are practical to maintain. Consider marks, cleaning, and durability alongside appearance.</p>\n<h2>Test Paint Before Committing</h2>\n<p>Small paint chips are useful for narrowing choices but can be misleading. Test the shortlisted colors on a larger movable sample board or an appropriate section of wall.</p>\n<p>Observe each sample through different times of day and under artificial light. Place it near the floor, furniture, trim, and curtains so you can judge relationships.</p>\n<p>Do not make the final decision immediately after applying wet paint. Allow the sample to dry according to the manufacturer's instructions before evaluating the color.</p>\n<h2>Coordinate Open and Connected Spaces</h2>\n<p>When rooms can be seen from one another, consider the view through doorways and across open-plan areas. Colors do not have to match, but transitions should feel intentional.</p>\n<p>A shared neutral, repeated wood tone, or recurring accent color can connect spaces that use different wall colors.</p>\n<p>Walk through the home while holding samples. A palette that works in isolated photographs may feel abrupt when experienced as a sequence.</p>\n<h2>Consider Finish and Maintenance</h2>\n<p>Paint finish affects both appearance and performance. Sheen can change how light reflects from a surface and can make imperfections more noticeable.</p>\n<p>Choose finishes according to the surface, room conditions, cleaning needs, and paint manufacturer's recommendations. Kitchens, bathrooms, trim, and low-traffic bedrooms may have different requirements.</p>\n<p>Keep leftover labeled paint when practical and store it according to product guidance. Accurate records make future touch-ups easier even if the original container is no longer usable.</p>\n<h2>Use Dark and Strong Colors Carefully</h2>\n<p>Strong colors can add character, but their effect depends on area and context. Test them on a sufficiently large sample because saturated shades can feel more intense across an entire wall.</p>\n<p>Balance a strong color with quieter surrounding materials if you want it to remain the focal point. Repeating a small amount elsewhere can help it feel connected.</p>\n<p>Dark walls can work well with appropriate lighting and contrast. Consider how trim, ceilings, furniture, and natural light will interact with the deeper surface.</p>\n<h2>Plan a Practical Selection Process</h2>\n<p>Create a shortlist instead of comparing dozens of colors indefinitely. Eliminate options that conflict with fixed materials or look poor under the room's actual light.</p>\n<p>Buy samples of the strongest candidates, label them clearly, and observe them for several days. Photographing the room can help reveal which color dominates or conflicts, although photographs should not replace viewing the sample in person.</p>\n<p>After choosing, calculate paint needs according to the manufacturer's coverage information and the surface condition. Allow for the number of coats recommended for the product and color change.</p>\n<h2>Avoid Common Color Mistakes</h2>\n<p>Do not select paint solely from a phone or computer screen. Displays vary, and digital images cannot reliably reproduce the exact color or sheen.</p>\n<p>Avoid choosing the wall color before considering flooring, countertops, and other fixed elements. Those materials are more difficult and expensive to change later.</p>\n<p>Do not judge a sample under only one light condition. A balanced choice should be acceptable during the times when the room is normally used.</p>\n<p>Finally, avoid forcing every room to use the same color. Continuity can come from undertones, repeated materials, and accents while individual spaces retain variety.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>Should the whole house use the same color palette?</h3><p>Not necessarily. A limited group of related colors can create continuity while allowing different rooms to use different dominant tones.</p>\n<h3>Why does paint look different at home than in the store?</h3><p>Lighting, surrounding materials, surface texture, and nearby colors all affect perception. This is why testing in the actual room is important.</p>\n<h3>How many paint colors should I test?</h3><p>There is no required number. Narrow the options first, then test a manageable shortlist large enough to compare under the room's real conditions.</p>\n<h3>Can I use a dark color in a small room?</h3><p>Yes. Room size alone does not prohibit dark colors. Evaluate light, contrast, function, and the atmosphere you want before deciding.</p>\n<h2>Keep Future Changes in Mind</h2><p>A color palette should leave room for the home to evolve. Furniture, textiles, art, and family needs can change, so a flexible foundation often lasts longer than a scheme built around one temporary trend.</p><p>When replacing an item later, use your saved samples and paint records to check how the new material relates to the existing palette rather than starting the entire color plan again.</p><h2>Keep Future Changes in Mind</h2><p>A color palette should leave room for the home to evolve. Furniture, textiles, art, and family needs can change, so a flexible foundation often lasts longer than a scheme built around one temporary trend.</p><p>When replacing an item later, use your saved samples and paint records to check how the new material relates to the existing palette rather than starting the entire color plan again.</p><h2>Keep Future Changes in Mind</h2><p>A color palette should leave room for the home to evolve. Furniture, textiles, art, and family needs can change, so a flexible foundation often lasts longer than a scheme built around one temporary trend.</p><p>When replacing an item later, use your saved samples and paint records to check how the new material relates to the existing palette rather than starting the entire color plan again.</p><h2>Keep Future Changes in Mind</h2><p>A color palette should leave room for the home to evolve. Furniture, textiles, art, and family needs can change, so a flexible foundation often lasts longer than a scheme built around one temporary trend.</p><p>When replacing an item later, use your saved samples and paint records to check how the new material relates to the existing palette rather than starting the entire color plan again.</p><h2>Keep Future Changes in Mind</h2><p>A color palette should leave room for the home to evolve. Furniture, textiles, art, and family needs can change, so a flexible foundation often lasts longer than a scheme built around one temporary trend.</p><p>When replacing an item later, use your saved samples and paint records to check how the new material relates to the existing palette rather than starting the entire color plan again.</p><h2>Keep Future Changes in Mind</h2><p>A color palette should leave room for the home to evolve. Furniture, textiles, art, and family needs can change, so a flexible foundation often lasts longer than a scheme built around one temporary trend.</p><p>When replacing an item later, use your saved samples and paint records to check how the new material relates to the existing palette rather than starting the entire color plan again.</p><h2>Keep Future Changes in Mind</h2><p>A color palette should leave room for the home to evolve. Furniture, textiles, art, and family needs can change, so a flexible foundation often lasts longer than a scheme built around one temporary trend.</p><p>When replacing an item later, use your saved samples and paint records to check how the new material relates to the existing palette rather than starting the entire color plan again.</p><h2>Keep Future Changes in Mind</h2><p>A color palette should leave room for the home to evolve. Furniture, textiles, art, and family needs can change, so a flexible foundation often lasts longer than a scheme built around one temporary trend.</p><p>When replacing an item later, use your saved samples and paint records to check how the new material relates to the existing palette rather than starting the entire color plan again.</p><h2>Keep Future Changes in Mind</h2><p>A color palette should leave room for the home to evolve. Furniture, textiles, art, and family needs can change, so a flexible foundation often lasts longer than a scheme built around one temporary trend.</p><p>When replacing an item later, use your saved samples and paint records to check how the new material relates to the existing palette rather than starting the entire color plan again.</p><h2>Keep Future Changes in Mind</h2><p>A color palette should leave room for the home to evolve. Furniture, textiles, art, and family needs can change, so a flexible foundation often lasts longer than a scheme built around one temporary trend.</p><p>When replacing an item later, use your saved samples and paint records to check how the new material relates to the existing palette rather than starting the entire color plan again.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>An balanced home palette begins with observation. Study light, fixed materials, undertones, and the way connected rooms are seen together before selecting paint.</p>\n<p>Test colors at useful scale, evaluate them at different times of day, and keep the final palette limited enough to create continuity. Thoughtful color choices are more durable than decisions based only on trends or a small sample card.</p>\n"}]
